WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.070
Secure telemetry begins by treating
observability as a security-relevant data flow.

2
00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.967
Define meaningful events before collecting
fields,

3
00:00:08.047 --> 00:00:11.428
then map only the identity, tenant,
resource, action, outcome,

4
00:00:11.508 --> 00:00:15.639
and correlation facts that serve an
operational, security, or audit purpose.

5
00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:20.164
Structured logging makes those facts
consistent and preserves record boundaries,

6
00:00:20.244 --> 00:00:22.373
but it does not make an unsafe value safe.

7
00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:25.708
Logging everything in JSON is still
indiscriminate logging.

8
00:00:25.788 --> 00:00:27.291
Minimize before redacting.

9
00:00:27.371 --> 00:00:31.377
Keep credentials and authentication
material out of every telemetry path,

10
00:00:31.457 --> 00:00:34.149
including logs, traces, and hosted error
reports.

11
00:00:34.229 --> 00:00:38.048
Apply deliberate policy to identifiers,
personal data, customer text,

12
00:00:38.128 --> 00:00:40.882
network attributes, headers, and request
content.

13
00:00:40.962 --> 00:00:43.467
Redaction is defense in depth for known
fields;

14
00:00:43.547 --> 00:00:45.738
hashing is not automatic anonymization.

15
00:00:45.818 --> 00:00:49.449
Keep request-controlled values inside
bounded structured fields,

16
00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:52.659
while event names and severity remain
server-controlled.

17
00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:56.182
Downstream viewers and exports must render
those fields safely.

18
00:00:56.262 --> 00:00:59.204
Evidence is most useful when outcomes
remain distinct.

19
00:00:59.284 --> 00:01:03.166
Authentication failure, validation
rejection, authorization denial,

20
00:01:03.246 --> 00:01:07.753
infrastructure error, and successful
privileged action convey different meaning.

21
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:12.153
Correlation identifiers connect related
work but do not authenticate a subject.

22
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:16.490
Client-facing errors should reveal less
than protected internal diagnostics,

23
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.701
while useful stack traces can be retained
under suitable access and retention

24
00:01:20.781 --> 00:01:21.345
controls.

25
00:01:21.425 --> 00:01:25.682
Security records and disposable debug
output may require different schemas and

26
00:01:25.762 --> 00:01:26.951
delivery guarantees.

27
00:01:27.031 --> 00:01:29.661
Finally, protect and test the complete
pipeline.

28
00:01:29.741 --> 00:01:33.497
Define who can read telemetry, how long it
remains, how deletion works,

29
00:01:33.577 --> 00:01:35.580
and what happens when exporters fail.

30
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.167
Bound queues, volume, and retries so
observability cannot exhaust the application.

31
00:01:40.247 --> 00:01:44.379
Test the actual serialized records for
required evidence and forbidden data,

32
00:01:44.459 --> 00:01:48.341
and verify behavior through transport and
presentation where possible.

33
00:01:48.421 --> 00:01:53.053
The best security telemetry reconstructs
important behavior while refusing to become

34
00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:55.700
a copy of the application's most sensitive
data.
